# Executor notes (2026-09-12)
- Classification of the ASan truncate arm: the child died with SIGABRT (signal 6) because AddressSanitizer aborts after printing its DEADLYSIGNAL report; the report itself is "AddressSanitizer: BUS on unknown address ... The signal is caused by a READ memory access" in memchr::arch::aarch64::neon::memchr::One::find_raw called from fff_grep::searcher::glue::SliceByLine<PlainTextMatcher, PlainTextSink>::run <- Searcher::search_slice <- fff_search::grep::perform_grep (rayon worker). The harness line "NO fault observed" under ASan is therefore a misclassification by the harness (it only checks for 10/11); the non-ASan truncate arms above died with SIGBUS (signal 10) directly.
- Mechanism of the PlainText (workspace) violations: PlainTextSink::matched builds line_content with String::from_utf8_lossy(display_bytes).into_owned() (grep.rs:750; RegexSink :832, AhoCorasickSink :909 are identical). from_utf8_lossy validates the slice and returns Cow::Borrowed when valid; into_owned then copies the bytes a second time. Both reads go through a &[u8] over the live mapping, so a write(2) landing between the validation read and the copy yields a String whose bytes fail from_utf8. The from_utf8_unchecked route (grep.rs:1710, fuzzy_grep_search) only widens the window (whole-file validation once, per-line copies later), which is why the fuzzy arm shows ~49% invalid lines and the plain arm ~0.0002%.
- Mapping confirmed live in-process by vmmap ("mapped file ... r--/r-x SM=ALI .../big2.txt", 4 regions) and by the picker's cache budget (cached_count=4, cached_bytes=16777152) after the first grep; the below-threshold control (8190-byte files, slow read path) stayed clean in both modes with the same writer.
